    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"15-minute city\" concept, while popular for its promise of accessible urban living, has faced several criticisms. Some argue it mainly benefits wealthier residents as affordable public transportation options may be limited within such small, walkable areas, hence excluding low-income families. Others point out the potential for increased congestion due to reduced parking spaces and limited traffic flow. There's also a concern about gentrification, as higher living costs in an ultra-compact urban layout could displace long-term, lower-income residents. Finally, skeptics question the efficacy of implementing such a compact urban design in sprawling cities where resources are scarce and logistical challenges are significant.",
      "source": "target",
